DESCRIPTION:---------------\nLab: Attack Lab\n---------------\n\n \n\nThe 
 Attack Lab: Understanding Buffer Overflow Bugs\n--------------------------
 ------------------------\n\n \n\nThis assignment asks you to construct fi
 ve different attacks across two programs\, each containing distinct securi
 ty vulnerabilities. Below is a brief overview\, but you should:\n\n* Watch
  the AttackLab tutorial video\n\n* Download the PDF included in your GitHu
 b repository and follow it as you work through the lab\n\nWhat you will le
 arn\n\nBy completing this lab\, you will:\n\n* Explore several techniques 
 attackers use to exploit poorly protected programs\, especially those vuln
 erable to buffer overflows.\n\n* Gain insight into how to write more secur
 e code and how compilers and operating systems help reduce vulnerability.\
 n\n* Deepen your understanding of the x86‑64 stack layout and calling co
 nventions.\n\n* Learn more about how x86‑64 instructions are encoded.\n\
 n* Build additional experience using debugging tools such as GDB and OBJDU
 MP.\n\nYou should also review Sections 3.10.3 and 3.10.4 of the CS:APP3e t
 extbook as reference material.\n\nImportant note:\nThis lab provides hands
 -on exposure to techniques used to exploit security flaws in operating sys
 tems and network servers. The goal is to help you understand how programs 
 behave at runtime and how these weaknesses arise so that you can avoid the
 m in your own systems code. These techniques must not be used for any unau
 thorized access to computing resources.\n\n \n\nLogistics\n\nThis is an i
 ndividual assignment. Your target programs are custom‑generated for you.
 \n\nGetting the files\n\nAfter cloning your GitHub repository\, consult th
 e README.md for setup instructions.\n\nWhat to submit\n\nSubmit your work 
 by adding the required solution files to your repository\, committing\, an
 d pushing them to GitHub.\n\nYou must produce five input files correspondi
 ng to the five phases shown in Figure 1 of the lab writeup. These files mu
 st be placed in your target directory and named exactly:\n\n* ctarget.l1.t
 xt\n\n* ctarget.l2.txt\n\n* ctarget.l3.txt\n\n* rtarget.l2.txt\n\n* rtarge
 t.l3.txt\n\nFor example\, if your target directory is target4\, your files
  should be named:\n\n* target4/ctarget.l1.txt\n\n* target4/ctarget.l2.txt\
 n\n* target4/ctarget.l3.txt\n\n* target4/rtarget.l2.txt\n\n* target4/rtarg
 et.l3.txt
