X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://instructu
 re-uploads.s3.amazonaws.com/account_107720000000000001/attachments/7403587
 6/dp_app.css"><h2>Lab: Attack Lab</h2>\n<div id="intro" class="box py-3 ge
 neralbox boxaligncenter">\n<div class="no-overflow">\n<p>&nbsp\;</p>\n<div
  title="Page 1">\n<h3>The Attack Lab: Understanding Buffer Overflow Bugs</
 h3>\n<p>&nbsp\;</p>\n<p>This assignment asks you to construct five differe
 nt attacks across two programs\, each containing distinct security vulnera
 bilities. Below is a brief overview\, but you should:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Watch
  the AttackLab tutorial video</li>\n<li>Download the PDF included in your 
 GitHub repository and follow it as you work through the lab</li>\n</ul>\n<
 p>What you will learn</p>\n<p>By completing this lab\, you will:</p>\n<ul>
 \n<li>Explore several techniques attackers use to exploit poorly protected
  programs\, especially those vulnerable to buffer overflows.</li>\n<li>Gai
 n insight into how to write more secure code and how compilers and operati
 ng systems help reduce vulnerability.</li>\n<li>Deepen your understanding 
 of the x86‑64 stack layout and calling conventions.</li>\n<li>Learn more
  about how x86‑64 instructions are encoded.</li>\n<li>Build additional e
 xperience using debugging tools such as GDB and OBJDUMP.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Y
 ou should also review Sections 3.10.3 and 3.10.4 of the CS:APP3e textbook 
 as reference material.</p>\n<p><strong>Important note:</strong><br>This la
 b provides hands-on exposure to techniques used to exploit security flaws 
 in operating systems and network servers. The goal is to help you understa
 nd how programs behave at runtime and how these weaknesses arise so that y
 ou can avoid them in your own systems code. These techniques must not be u
 sed for any unauthorized access to computing resources.</p>\n<p>&nbsp\;</p
 >\n<p><strong>Logistics</strong></p>\n<p>This is an individual assignment.
  Your target programs are custom‑generated for you.</p>\n<p><strong>Gett
 ing the files</strong></p>\n<p>After cloning your GitHub repository\, cons
 ult the <code>README.md</code> for setup instructions.</p>\n<p><strong>Wha
 t to submit</strong></p>\n<p>Submit your work by adding the required solut
 ion files to your repository\, committing\, and pushing them to GitHub.</p
 >\n<p>You must produce five input files corresponding to the five phases s
 hown in Figure 1 of the lab writeup. These files must be placed in your ta
 rget directory and named exactly:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>ctarget.l1.txt</cod
 e></li>\n<li><code>ctarget.l2.txt</code></li>\n<li><code>ctarget.l3.txt</c
 ode></li>\n<li><code>rtarget.l2.txt</code></li>\n<li><code>rtarget.l3.txt<
 /code></li>\n</ul>\n<p>For example\, if your target directory is <code>tar
 get4</code>\, your files should be named:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>target4/cta
 rget.l1.txt</code></li>\n<li><code>target4/ctarget.l2.txt</code></li>\n<li
 ><code>target4/ctarget.l3.txt</code></li>\n<li><code>target4/rtarget.l2.tx
 t</code></li>\n<li><code>target4/rtarget.l3.txt</code></li>\n</ul>\n</div>
 \n</div>\n</div><script src="https://instructure-uploads.s3.amazonaws.com/
 account_107720000000000001/attachments/74035875/dp_app.js"></script>
