 6/dp_app.css"><p><strong><span>Homework 9 is due: 11:59 PM Wednesday\, Oct
  28th.&nbsp\;</span></strong></p>\n<p>All HW is completed in Jupyter noteb
 ooks (.ipynb file).&nbsp\;&nbsp\;</p>\n<p><span><a class="inline_disabled"
  href="https://coding.csel.io/hub/user-redirect/git-pull?repo=https%3A%2F%
 2Fgithub.com%2Fcsci3022%2Ffa26-student&amp\;urlpath=lab%2Ftree%2Ffa26-stud
 ent%2Fhw%2Fhw09%2Fhw09.ipynb&amp\;branch=main" target="_blank">Use this li
 nk to access HW 9</a><span class="external_link_icon" role="presentation">
 <span class="screenreader-only">Links to an external site.</span></span><s
 pan class="external_link_icon" role="presentation"><span class="screenread
 er-only">Links to an external site.</span></span><span class="external_lin
 k_icon" role="presentation"><span class="screenreader-only">Links to an ex
 ternal site.</span></span>&nbsp\;</span>(this will open the assignment in 
 the CSCI JupyterHub coding environment).&nbsp\;&nbsp\;</p>\n<p>&nbsp\;</p>
 \n<p><strong>Grading</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>\n<p>Grading is broken down i
 nto autograded answers and manually graded answers:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>For man
 ually graded answers you must show and explain all steps.&nbsp\;&nbsp\;</l
 i>\n<li>For autograded answers\, the results of your code are compared to 
 provided and/or hidden tests. &nbsp\;The visible tests are mainly sanity c
 hecks. For example\, a sanity check might verify that the answer you enter
 ed is a number as expected\, and not a word. The hidden tests generally ch
 eck for correctness\, and are invisible to students while they are doing t
 he assignment (hidden tests are run once the assignment has been submitted
  to Gradescope).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>&nbsp\;</p>\n<p><strong>Sub
 mission Instructions</strong>:</p>\n<p>After running the final cell in the
  notebook provided at the link above\, 2 files will be created: a zip file
  and pdf file. &nbsp\;You can download them using the links provided in th
 e notebook OR by finding them in the same folder where the HW1 Juptyer not
 ebook resides in your JuptyerHub.&nbsp\; &nbsp\;</p>\n<p>To receive credit
  on this assignment\,<span>&nbsp\;</span><strong>you must submit BOTH of t
 hese files</strong><br><strong>to their respective Gradescope portals</str
 ong>:&nbsp\;</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Homework 9 Autograded<span>&nbsp\;</sp
 an></strong>: Submit the zip file that is output by jupyter notebook to th
 e HW 10 Autograded assignment in Gradescope.</li>\n<li><strong>Homework 9 
 Manually Graded:<span>&nbsp\;</span><span>&nbsp\;</span></strong>Submit th
 e pdf that is output by the juptyer notebook to the HW 10 Manually Graded 
 assignment in Gradescope.&nbsp\; You must match the pages in your PDF to t
 he problems in Gradescope to receive full credit.&nbsp\;&nbsp\;</li>\n</ul
 >\n<p>Submissions that are not submitted to Gradescope by the deadline wil
 l not be counted for credit.</p>\n<p>&nbsp\;</p>\n<p><span><strong>Collabo
 ration Policy</strong></span></p>\n<p>Data science is a collaborative acti
 vity. &nbsp\;However a key step in learning and retention is creating solu
 tions on your own.&nbsp\;&nbsp\;Below are examples of acceptable vs unacce
 ptable use of resources and collaboration when doing HW assignments in CSC
 I 3022.</p>\n<p><br>The following would be some **examples of cheating** w
 hen working on HW assignments in CSCI 3022. &nbsp\;Any of these constitute
  a **violation of the course's collaboration policy and will result in an 
 F in the course and a trip to the honor council**. &nbsp\;&nbsp\;</p>\n<ul
 >\n<li>Consulting web pages that may have a solution to a given homework p
 roblem or one similar is cheating. &nbsp\;However\, consulting the class n
 otes\, and web pages that explain the material taught in class but do NOT 
 show a solution to the homework problem in question are permissible to vie
 w. &nbsp\;Clearly\, there's a fuzzy line here between a valid use of resou
 rces and cheating. To avoid this line\, one should merely consult the cour
 se notes\, the course textbook\, and references that contain syntax and/or
  formulas.</li>\n<li>Copying a segment of code or math solution of three l
 ines or more from another student from a printout\, handwritten copy\, or 
 by looking at their computer screen</li>\n<li>Sharing a segment of code or
  math solution of three lines or more with another student</li>\n<li>&nbsp
 \;Taking a copy of another student's work (or a solution found online) and
  then editing that copy</li>\n<li>Reading someone else’s solution to a p
 roblem on the HW before writing your own.</li>\n<li>Asking someone to writ
 e all or part of a program or solution for you.</li>\n<li>Asking someone e
 lse for the code necessary to fix the error for you\, other than for simpl
 e syntactical errors<br><br></li>\n</ul>\n<p>On the other hand\, the follo
 wing are some examples of things which would NOT usually be<br>considered 
 to be cheating:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Working on a HW problem on your own first a
 nd then discussing with a classmate a particular part in the problem solut
 ion where you are stuck. &nbsp\;After clarifying any questions you then co
 ntinue to write your solution independently.</li>\n<li>Asking someone (or 
 searching online) how a particular construct in the language works.</li>\n
 <li>Asking someone (or searching online) how to formulate a particular con
 struct in the language.</li>\n<li>Asking someone for help in finding an er
 ror in your program. &nbsp\;</li>\n<li>Asking someone why a particular con
 struct does not work as you expected in a given program.<br>&nbsp\; &nbsp\
 ;</li>\n</ul>\n<p>To test whether you are truly doing your own work and re
 taining what you've learned you should be able to easily reproduce from sc
 ratch and explain a HW solution that was your own when asked in office hou
 rs by a TA/Instructor or on a quiz/exam. &nbsp\;&nbsp\;</p>\n<p>If you hav
 e difficulty in formulating the general solution to a problem on your own\
 , or<br>you have difficulty in translating that general solution into a pr
 ogram\, it is advisable to see<br>your instructor or teaching assistant ra
 ther than another student as this situation can easily<br>lead to a\, poss
 ibly inadvertent\, cheating situation.</p>\n<p>We are here to help! &nbsp\
 ;Visit HW Hours and/or post questions on Piazza!</p><script src="https://i
 nstructure-uploads.s3.amazonaws.com/account_107720000000000001/attachments
 /74035875/dp_app.js"></script>
